---
// THE TOOL PAGE. One option at a time: pick a tab and the page becomes only
// that procedure. Everything conceptual — why, what your device allows, how it
// goes wrong — lives on /generate-your-seed, and is not repeated here.
//
// NO TABS FOR OPTION 4. "Let the device do it" is a sentence, not a procedure.
//
// NOTHING IS TYPED, NOTHING IS STORED. There is no input on this page and
// there is not going to be one: the sequence of numbers a reader rolls IS
// their seed in another alphabet. The tabs only show and hide server-rendered
// panels — no state, no storage, no network.
//
// The roll tally that used to live here was removed: on the device options the
// device shows its own running count and hash, and on option 1 the unit is
// words, not throws. It answered a question nobody in either procedure asks.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RuleRef from '../components/RuleRef.astro';
import {
  diceVerified, methods, deviceProcedures, walkStepsFor,
  ratedDevicesForMethod, DICE_METHOD_KEYS, seedLengths,
} from '../data/dice.js';
import { seedCosts, tableRowCount, DICE_PER_WORD } from '../data/dice-table.js';

// Option 4 has no procedure, so the tabs are exactly the doing options.
const tabs = methods.filter((m) => DICE_METHOD_KEYS.includes(m.key));
const procsFor = (key) => deviceProcedures.filter((p) => (p.options || []).includes(key));
const trim = (n) => n.replace(' (BTC-only)', '').replace('Blockstream ', '').replace('Foundation ', '');
const devicesFor = (key) => ratedDevicesForMethod(key).map((d) => trim(d.name));
// The two lengths this guide's worksheet covers, by POSITION rather than by a
// typed word count. Everything the page says about length — the counts, the bit
// figures, the vendor roll minimums — comes off these, so the copy cannot drift
// from the data. The page used to say "ninety-nine throws" in words: a count
// with a data file behind it and no link to it.
const shortLen = seedLengths[0];
const longLen = seedLengths[seedLengths.length - 1];
const longCost = seedCosts.find((c) => c.words === 24);
const shortCost = seedCosts.find((c) => c.words === 12);
---
<Base
  title="Roll your own seed — the procedures · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Step-by-step procedures for putting your own randomness into a Bitcoin seed, on the hardware you own. Pick your method; the page shows only that one."
  verified={diceVerified}
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Hardware &amp; services · procedures</p>
    <h1>Roll your own seed</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Pick a method and this page becomes only that method. If you have not decided yet, or you are not sure
      what your device allows, start at <a href="/learn/generate-your-seed">where your seed's randomness comes from</a>.
    </p>

    {/* SEED LENGTH, BEFORE THE PROCEDURES. Every panel below counts in words —
        "23 for a 24-word seed, or 11 for a 12-word one" — and until this block
        existed the page never said what those two numbers were, that they are
        the only two, or that the reader has to pick one before starting. A
        beginner met the choice already made and never explained.
        It sits on the TOOL page rather than the lesson because it is a decision
        the procedure cannot start without, which is the same reason "print the
        table first" is here. Both lengths, both bit figures and the roll counts
        derive from seedLengths. */}
    <div class="callout" role="note">
      <span class="label">First, pick a length — {shortLen.words} words or {longLen.words}</span>
      <p>
        Those are the two you will actually be offered, and every wallet worth owning takes both. The
        difference is how much randomness sits behind them: <strong>{shortLen.words} words carry
        {' '}{shortLen.target} bits and {longLen.words} words carry {longLen.target}</strong> — and both are
        so far past anything that could be guessed that neither is the weak point in your setup. What really
        differs is the work: {longLen.words} words is more to roll, more to write down, more to check, and
        more to copy onto metal later.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        <strong>We suggest {longLen.words} if you are doing this by hand anyway.</strong> The extra effort is
        one more page of rolling, once, ever — and the margin is free after that. But
        {' '}{shortLen.words} is a real answer, not a compromise, and it is the easier one to get right.
        <strong> Decide before you throw anything</strong>, because it sets how many words you roll and which
        screen you pick on the device; changing your mind halfway means starting again. <em>(The standard
        does allow a few in-between lengths — you may see a {shortLen.words} / 18 / {longLen.words} menu on a
        Coldcard — but almost nothing uses them, and our worksheet is built for these two.)</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout danger" role="note">
      <span class="label">🛑 This page will never ask what you rolled</span>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        There is no box to type your throws into, and there is not going to be one. <strong>The list of numbers
        you roll IS your seed</strong>, written in a different alphabet — anyone who has it has your coins.
        <RuleRef key="never-digital" /> says key material never touches anything digital, and we are not making
        an exception for our own page. Your throws belong on <strong>paper</strong> — our printable worksheet is
        made for exactly that, and it becomes part of your seed the moment you mark it — or straight into your
        <strong>hardware wallet</strong>. Never onto a screen, and never into this one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Choose a method" id="opt-tabs">
      {tabs.map((m, i) => (
        <button
          type="button" role="tab" class="tab" data-opt={m.key}
          id={`tab-${m.key}`} aria-controls={`panel-${m.key}`}
          aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}
        >
          <span class="tab-n">Option {m.n}</span>
          <span class="tab-l">{m.label}</span>
          {/* The CHOOSING line and the device list, both on the tab. Before this
              the tabs carried a number and a label, so picking between three
              procedures meant opening each one and reading a panel — and the
              single most decisive fact, whether your own hardware can do it at
              all, was buried inside. Both come from dice.js, so a tab can never
              claim a device the procedure does not cover. */}
          <span class="tab-p">{m.pick}</span>
          <span class="tab-d">
            <span class="tab-dk">Devices</span>
            {devicesFor(m.key).length ? devicesFor(m.key).join(' · ') : 'None of the ones we rate.'}
          </span>
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>

    {tabs.map((m, i) => (
      <div
        class="panel" role="tabpanel" data-panel={m.key}
        id={`panel-${m.key}`} aria-labelledby={`tab-${m.key}`}
        hidden={i !== 0}
      >
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>{m.label}</h2>
          <p class="p-tag">{m.tagline}</p>
          <p>{m.how}</p>
          <p class="p-meta">
            <span class="p-k">Devices we rate that can do this</span>
            {devicesFor(m.key).length ? devicesFor(m.key).join(' · ') : 'None of them.'}
          </p>
          <p class="p-meta">
            <span class="p-k">What it costs you</span>
            {m.key === 'sovereign'
              ? `About ${longCost.throws} throws and ${longCost.flips} coin flips for ${longLen.words} words (${shortCost.throws} and ${shortCost.flips} for ${shortLen.words}), each read off a printed table by hand. Set aside an evening.`
              : m.key === 'dice-only'
                ? `${longLen.minRollsD6} throws for ${longLen.words} words (${shortLen.minRollsD6} for ${shortLen.words}), entered straight into the device. No table, no transcription.`
                : 'As many throws as you feel like. There is no minimum, and more is simply better.'}
          </p>

          {m.key === 'sovereign' && (
            <div class="callout" role="note">
              <span class="label">Print the table first</span>
              <p style="margin:0">
                All {tableRowCount.toLocaleString()} words, reachable from {DICE_PER_WORD} throws and a coin
                flip. Print it or open it on a second screen before you start —
                <a href="/dice-word-table"> the dice → word table</a>, or
                <a href="/dice-word-table.pdf" download> download the PDF</a>. It contains no secret: it is the
                standard wordlist in a fixed order, and anyone can regenerate it.
              </p>
            </div>
          )}

          <h3>The steps</h3>
          {/* PER METHOD, never shared. Option 1 hands the device finished words
              and asks for the last one; options 2 and 3 hand it throws. Rendering
              one procedure under the other told option-1 readers to enter their
              rolls into the device four lines under the sentence saying their
              randomness never touches a machine. walkStepsFor() is what makes
              that combination unreachable. */}
          <ol class="wsteps">
            {walkStepsFor(m.key).map((s) => (<li><strong>{s.t}.</strong> {s.d}</li>))}
          </ol>

          <h3>On your device</h3>
          {procsFor(m.key).map((p) => (
            <div class={`dev${p.partial ? ' is-partial' : ''}`}>
              <p class="dev-h">{p.label}{p.partial && <span class="dev-flag">with an asterisk</span>}</p>
              <p class="dev-path mono">{p.path.join('  →  ')}</p>
              {p.pathNote && <p class="dev-note">{p.pathNote}</p>}
              {p.caution && <p class="dev-caution">{p.caution}</p>}
              {p.source && (
                <p class="dev-src">
                  <a href={p.source.url} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">{p.source.label} ↗</a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/learn/generate-your-seed">← Why, and what your device allows</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/checklist">Your setup checklist</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  // Show/hide only. No state is read or written anywhere — the panels are all
  // server-rendered and the tab merely toggles `hidden`.
  const bar = document.getElementById('opt-tabs');
  if (bar) {
    const tabs = Array.from(bar.querySelectorAll('button[data-opt]'));
    const panels = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('[data-panel]'));
    const select = (key) => {
      tabs.forEach((t) => t.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(t.dataset.opt === key)));
      panels.forEach((p) => { p.hidden = p.dataset.panel !== key; });
    };
    bar.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const t = e.target && e.target.closest ? e.target.closest('button[data-opt]') : null;
      if (t) select(t.dataset.opt);
    });
    // Deep link: /roll-your-own-seed#enrich opens that option directly. The
    // checklist sends readers here with the option their own hardware can
    // actually do, so landing on option 1 regardless would undo the point.
    // Unknown fragments are ignored rather than blanking every panel.
    const fromHash = () => {
      const k = (location.hash || '').replace('#', '');
      if (tabs.some((t) => t.dataset.opt === k)) select(k);
    };
    fromHash();
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', fromHash);
  }
</script>

<style>
  .tabs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .6rem; margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  .tab { flex: 1 1 14rem; text-align: left; cursor: pointer; font: inherit;
    background: transparent; color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: .8rem 1rem; }
  .tab:hover { border-color: var(--accent); }
  .tab[aria-selected='true'] { border-color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft); }
  .tab-n { display: block; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .07em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); }
  .tab-l { display: block; font-weight: 600; margin-top: .15rem; }
  /* The tab is now a CARD you choose from, so the three have to be the same
     height or the shortest reads as the lesser option. Flex column + the
     device line pushed to the bottom with margin-top:auto does that without
     a fixed height, which would clip on a narrow phone. */
  .tab { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .tab-p { display: block; margin-top: .4rem; font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--muted); font-weight: 400; }
  /* MINIMISED, not hidden. Small mono type at the foot of the card — enough to
     scan for your own device, not enough to compete with the choosing line.
     Deliberately not a <details>: interactive content cannot nest inside a
     <button>, and turning the tab into a div would cost the keyboard semantics
     the tablist depends on. */
  .tab-d { display: block; margin-top: auto; padding-top: .55rem; font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .68rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--faint, var(--muted)); }
  .tab-dk { display: block; font-size: .6rem; letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: .1rem; }
  .tab[aria-selected='true'] .tab-p { color: var(--text); }
  .p-tag { color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; margin-top: -.4rem; }
  .p-meta { margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
  .p-k { display: block; font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: .1rem; }
  .wsteps li { margin-bottom: .8rem; }
  .dev { border-left: 3px solid var(--line); padding: .3rem 0 .3rem 1rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .dev.is-partial { border-left-color: var(--warn, #b7791f); }
  .dev-h { font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 .2rem; }
  .dev-flag { font-weight: 400; font-size: .75rem; color: var(--muted); margin-left: .5rem; }
  .dev-path { margin: 0 0 .35rem; font-size: .88rem; overflow-x: auto; }
  .dev-note, .dev-caution, .dev-src { margin: 0 0 .3rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .7rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 700; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .9rem; padding: .65rem 1rem;
    border-radius: .6rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .btn.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--text); border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .btn.ghost:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
</style>
